<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<!--
  Shellvis installer authoring. ONE package, and the scope is chosen while it runs.

  WHAT THIS FILE USED TO SAY, AND WHY IT WAS WRONG. It said Windows Installer fixes the
  install scope at BUILD time, so "pick the variant you want" could not be a checkbox
  inside one .msi, and it therefore built two packages from one source. That is not true.
  MSI 4.5 has Single Package Authoring: a package with ALLUSERS=2 and MSIINSTALLPERUSER
  installs per-user or per-machine depending on what the session sets, and the install-scope
  page of WixUI_Advanced sets it from what the user picked. Verified before this was
  written: Scope="perUserOrMachine" through WiX 5.0.2 produces exactly those two
  properties in the built package.

  So there is one download, and the choice is a page in the installer:

    per user     %LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\Shellvis, no elevation, no service
    per machine  %ProgramFiles%\Shellvis, elevation, the broker service selectable

  WHY THE SERVICE IS A FEATURE AND NOT A SECOND PACKAGE. The broker genuinely is optional:
  the application works without it and reports elevated operations as unavailable. That is a
  feature selection, which is what Windows Installer features are for. It is also why the
  scope choice and the service choice are two questions and not one; a service running as
  LocalSystem cannot exist in a per-user installation at all, so that feature switches
  itself off when the user picks per-user rather than offering something impossible.

  NO CUSTOM ACTIONS OF OUR OWN, and the qualifier is new. Nothing here runs code: the one
  thing that would have needed native code is the installing user's SID, which the broker's
  pipe ACL grants, and Windows Installer exposes the user's NAME as [LogonUser] and never
  their SID. The broker resolves the name itself.

  What the file cannot claim any more is that the PACKAGE contains no custom actions at all.
  The scope choice needs three, and every one of them is a type 51 SetProperty: it writes a
  property and does nothing else. No code of ours runs during an installation, which is the
  property that actually matters; a package that cannot leave a half-installed machine behind
  is still the point, and setting a property cannot.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:ui="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/ui">

  <!--
    A NEW UpgradeCode, because the product this replaces was two products. The two old
    packages had a code each, deliberately, so that installing one would not silently
    uninstall the other. A single package inherits neither cleanly, and a cross-context major
    upgrade (a per-user install removing a per-machine one, or the reverse) is exactly the
    sort of half-completed thing this package avoids everywhere else. So both old codes are
    detected below and the install stops with a sentence instead.
  -->
  <?define UpgradeGuid = "C4E7A2B9-3D18-4F5A-8E6C-0B9D71F4A238" ?>
  <?define LegacyUserGuid = "7A5C1E64-6C1F-4A83-9E5B-1D4F8B2A0C11" ?>
  <?define LegacyMachineGuid = "2F91B0D7-45AE-4C36-8B0E-9C7A6E5D3F22" ?>

  <Package Name="Shellvis"
           Manufacturer="imoes"
           Version="$(Version)"
           UpgradeCode="$(UpgradeGuid)"
           Scope="perUserOrMachine"
           Compressed="yes">

    <!--
      Refuse a downgrade rather than let it half-happen. The default behaviour without this
      element is to allow two versions side by side, which for an application that keeps
      state in one place under the profile is a way to have two builds fighting over it.
    -->
    <MajorUpgrade DowngradeErrorMessage="A newer version of Shellvis is already installed. Uninstall it first if you really want [ProductVersion]." />

    <!--
      The two packages this one replaces, detected rather than upgraded.
      Removing them from here would mean a per-user session uninstalling a per-machine
      product or the reverse, which Windows Installer does not do reliably across contexts.
      A refusal with a sentence is worth more than an upgrade that works four times out of
      five and leaves a service registered on the fifth.
    -->
    <Upgrade Id="$(LegacyUserGuid)">
      <UpgradeVersion Property="LEGACYUSER" Minimum="0.0.0" IncludeMinimum="yes" OnlyDetect="yes" />
    </Upgrade>

    <Upgrade Id="$(LegacyMachineGuid)">
      <UpgradeVersion Property="LEGACYMACHINE" Minimum="0.0.0" IncludeMinimum="yes" OnlyDetect="yes" />
    </Upgrade>

    <Launch Condition="NOT LEGACYUSER AND NOT LEGACYMACHINE"
            Message="An older Shellvis is installed as a separate product (the -user or -machine package). Uninstall it from Apps &amp; features first, then run this installer; your settings under %USERPROFILE%\.shellvis are not touched by either step." />

    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" />

    <Icon Id="ShellvisIcon" SourceFile="$(Stage)\Shellvis.Shell.exe" />
    <Property Id="ARPPRODUCTICON" Value="ShellvisIcon" />
    <Property Id="ARPURLINFOABOUT" Value="https://github.com/imoes/shellvis" />

    <!-- The licence has to be shown, not merely shipped: it is AGPL-3.0. -->
    <WixVariable Id="WixUILicenseRtf" Value="License.rtf" />

    <!--
      WixUI_FeatureTree, and the scope page is authored here rather than borrowed.

      WixUI_Advanced was tried first, because it ships an install-scope page. It puts that page
      behind an "Advanced" button, and the main "Install" button starts the installation
      immediately: measured twice, pressing Install ran three dialogs (welcome, progress, exit)
      and the choice never appeared, which is exactly the complaint that came back from the
      person installing it.

      Routing the button was tried and does not work. A NewDialog published at Ordering 0 for
      both Install buttons does land in the package's ControlEvent table, and Windows Installer
      still ran the built-in EndDialog at Ordering 2 instead: MSI treats an ordering of 0 as
      unspecified, and there is no ordering below 1 to claim. Fighting a foreign dialog set for
      the first click is not a thing to leave in a package.

      So the choice is a page of our own, inserted where every user passes through it. This
      file already authors a dialog for the speech model; a second one is the same idiom, and
      the sequencing below is on the main path by construction rather than by override.
    -->
    <ui:WixUI Id="WixUI_FeatureTree" />

    <!--
      The scope, as the user picked it. Secure="yes" so an unattended install can set it too:
      msiexec /i Shellvis.msi SCOPE=machine

      "user" is the default because it needs no elevation and touches nothing outside the
      profile. Note that on a managed desktop it may also be the choice that does not RUN:
      Defender's rule against low-prevalence executables blocks an unsigned binary under
      %LOCALAPPDATA%, and %ProgramFiles% is outside what that rule looks at. Measured on the
      machine this was built on, with a byte-identical binary allowed from one path and blocked
      from the other, which is worth knowing when choosing.
    -->
    <Property Id="SCOPE" Value="user" Secure="yes" />

    <!--
      The whole mechanism of Single Package Authoring is one property, and WHEN it is set
      matters as much as what it is set to.

      ALLUSERS stays at 2, which is what Scope="perUserOrMachine" put there. MSIINSTALLPERUSER
      is the switch: 1 means per-user, empty means per-machine. ALLUSERS must NOT be rewritten;
      doing that is how a dual-purpose package ends up half in one context and half in the
      other.

      The switch is thrown by the SCOPE PAGE, not by a scheduled action, and that is the second
      thing this got wrong. A SetProperty scheduled before LaunchConditions runs before any
      dialog has appeared, so it can only ever see the default; the same goes for the install
      folder, because CostFinalize in the UI sequence runs before the dialogs too. Both are
      therefore published from the page's Next button, which is also how the toolset's own
      scope dialog does it.

      What remains here is the SILENT path, which has no dialogs at all. There the caller
      supplies MSIINSTALLPERUSER on the command line and this maps it to the folder, in the
      execute sequence where it is the only thing that runs. It agrees with the page rather
      than fighting it: if the page chose per-machine the property is empty and this does
      nothing.
    -->
    <SetProperty Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Value="[LocalAppDataFolder]Programs\Shellvis"
                 Before="CostFinalize" Sequence="execute" Condition="MSIINSTALLPERUSER" />

    <!--
      WixUISupportPerUser and WixUISupportPerMachine are NOT set here, and neither is
      WIXUI_INSTALLDIR. All three already exist inside WixUI_Advanced: the support flags as
      virtual WixVariables defaulting to 1, which is what "offer both" means, and the install
      directory as a property pointing at INSTALLFOLDER. Redeclaring them is a link
      error, not a redundancy, so the absence is deliberate: both scopes are offered because
      nothing switched one off.
    -->


    <!--
      Which speech model dictation should use.

      Asked here rather than decided, because the answer costs the user a download between
      74 MB and 1.5 GB and some seconds of CPU per dictation, and because the right answer
      differs by machine: a laptop on a metered connection and a desktop on ethernet do not
      want the same one. Secure="yes" so it also works from the command line for an
      unattended install: msiexec /i Shellvis.msi WHISPERMODEL=medium

      The model is NOT shipped inside the MSI. At up to 1.5 GB it would be paid for by every
      user including those who never dictate, and GitHub refuses release assets over 100 MB, so
      the package could not carry it even if that were wanted. Shellvis fetches the chosen
      model on first use and says so in its console while it does.
    -->
    <Property Id="WHISPERMODEL" Value="medium" Secure="yes" />

    <UI>
      <!--
        The choice, on the main path. Between the licence and the feature tree, because the
        feature tree's contents DEPEND on it: the broker service exists in one scope and not
        in the other, so asking about features first would offer something that then vanishes.
      -->
      <Dialog Id="ScopeDlg" Width="370" Height="270" Title="[ProductName] Setup">
        <Control Id="BannerBitmap" Type="Bitmap" X="0" Y="0" Width="370" Height="44"
                 TabSkip="no" Text="WixUI_Bmp_Banner" />
        <Control Id="BannerLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="44" Width="370" Height="0" />
        <Control Id="BottomLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="234" Width="370" Height="0" />

        <Control Id="Title" Type="Text" X="15" Y="6" Width="220" Height="15" Transparent="yes"
                 NoPrefix="yes" Text="{\WixUI_Font_Title}Who is this installation for?" />

        <Control Id="Description" Type="Text" X="25" Y="23" Width="330" Height="15"
                 Transparent="yes" NoPrefix="yes"
                 Text="This decides where Shellvis goes and whether the broker can run." />

        <Control Id="ScopeGroup" Type="RadioButtonGroup" X="20" Y="58" Width="330" Height="130"
                 Property="SCOPE">
          <RadioButtonGroup Property="SCOPE">
            <RadioButton Value="user" X="0" Y="0" Width="325" Height="40"
                         Text="Just for me. Installs into my profile, needs no administrator, and cannot register the broker service. On a managed machine, note that security policy may refuse to run programs from a profile folder." />

            <RadioButton Value="machine" X="0" Y="48" Width="325" Height="40"
                         Text="For everyone on this machine. Installs into Program Files, needs administrator rights once, and can register the broker service so the elevated tools work." />
          </RadioButtonGroup>
        </Control>

        <Control Id="Note" Type="Text" X="20" Y="196" Width="330" Height="30"
                 Text="Either way your settings, notes and sessions stay in %USERPROFILE%\.shellvis and are untouched by installing or uninstalling." />

        <Control Id="Back" Type="PushButton" X="180" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17"
                 Text="!(loc.WixUIBack)" />

        <Control Id="Next" Type="PushButton" X="236" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17"
                 Default="yes" Text="!(loc.WixUINext)" />

        <Control Id="Cancel" Type="PushButton" X="304" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17"
                 Cancel="yes" Text="!(loc.WixUICancel)">
          <Publish Event="SpawnDialog" Value="CancelDlg" />
        </Control>
      </Dialog>

      <Dialog Id="WhisperDlg" Width="370" Height="270" Title="[ProductName] Setup">
        <Control Id="BannerBitmap" Type="Bitmap" X="0" Y="0" Width="370" Height="44"
                 TabSkip="no" Text="WixUI_Bmp_Banner" />
        <Control Id="BannerLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="44" Width="370" Height="0" />
        <Control Id="BottomLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="234" Width="370" Height="0" />

        <Control Id="Title" Type="Text" X="15" Y="6" Width="200" Height="15" Transparent="yes"
                 NoPrefix="yes" Text="{\WixUI_Font_Title}Speech recognition" />

        <Control Id="Description" Type="Text" X="25" Y="23" Width="330" Height="15"
                 Transparent="yes" NoPrefix="yes"
                 Text="Choose the model dictation recognises with. Everything runs on this machine." />

        <Control Id="Explanation" Type="Text" X="20" Y="55" Width="330" Height="24" Text="Windows' own recogniser is available without a download, but its German dictation is poor. A Whisper model is markedly better and is downloaded once, on first use." />

        <Control Id="ModelGroup" Type="RadioButtonGroup" X="20" Y="85" Width="330" Height="120"
                 Property="WHISPERMODEL" />

        <Control Id="Footnote" Type="Text" X="20" Y="208" Width="330" Height="24" Text="Nothing is sent anywhere at any point. You can change this later under voice.whisperModel in config.yaml." />

        <Control Id="Back" Type="PushButton" X="180" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17"
                 Text="!(loc.WixUIBack)" />

        <Control Id="Next" Type="PushButton" X="236" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17"
                 Default="yes" Text="!(loc.WixUINext)" />

        <Control Id="Cancel" Type="PushButton" X="304" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17"
                 Cancel="yes" Text="!(loc.WixUICancel)">
          <Publish Event="SpawnDialog" Value="CancelDlg" />
        </Control>
      </Dialog>

      <RadioButtonGroup Property="WHISPERMODEL">
        <RadioButton Value="medium" X="0" Y="0" Width="320" Height="16"
                     Text="Whisper medium - 1.5 GB. Recommended: gets German word endings right." />
        <RadioButton Value="small" X="0" Y="20" Width="320" Height="16"
                     Text="Whisper small - 465 MB. Three times faster, but guesses word endings." />
        <RadioButton Value="base" X="0" Y="40" Width="320" Height="16"
                     Text="Whisper base - 141 MB. Fast and small, stumbles on names." />
        <RadioButton Value="tiny" X="0" Y="60" Width="320" Height="16"
                     Text="Whisper tiny - 74 MB. Fastest, about as good as Windows' own." />
        <RadioButton Value="none" X="0" Y="80" Width="320" Height="16"
                     Text="None - use the Windows recogniser, download nothing." />
      </RadioButtonGroup>

      <!--
        Inserted between the feature tree and the confirmation page. The two overrides on
        the surrounding dialogs carry a higher Order than the ones WixUI_FeatureTree
        publishes, which is how a built-in dialog set is extended without forking it.
      -->
      <!--
        The two inserted pages, in the order a person meets them:

          LicenseAgreementDlg -> ScopeDlg -> CustomizeDlg -> WhisperDlg -> VerifyReadyDlg

        The overrides on the built-in dialogs carry a higher Order than the ones
        WixUI_FeatureTree publishes, which is how a built-in dialog set is extended without
        forking it. Order matters and 0 does not work: MSI treats an ordering of 0 as
        unspecified, which is what defeated the earlier attempt to route WixUI_Advanced's
        Install button.
      -->
      <Publish Dialog="LicenseAgreementDlg" Control="Next" Event="NewDialog" Value="ScopeDlg"
               Condition="LicenseAccepted = &quot;1&quot;" Order="10" />

      <Publish Dialog="ScopeDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="LicenseAgreementDlg" />

      <!--
        Next does four things, in this order, and every one of them has to be here rather than
        in a scheduled action: a dialog is the only place that runs after the user has chosen.

        1 and 2 throw the per-user switch and pick the matching folder.
        3 re-costs the directory. Without SetTargetPath the property changes and the directory
          table does not, so the files go where the default said and only the registry entries
          follow the choice.
        4 moves on, last, because a NewDialog ends the dialog and anything after it is lost.
      -->
      <Publish Dialog="ScopeDlg" Control="Next" Property="MSIINSTALLPERUSER" Value="1"
               Condition="SCOPE = &quot;user&quot;" Order="1" />

      <Publish Dialog="ScopeDlg" Control="Next" Property="MSIINSTALLPERUSER" Value="{}"
               Condition="SCOPE &lt;&gt; &quot;user&quot;" Order="1" />

      <Publish Dialog="ScopeDlg" Control="Next" Property="INSTALLFOLDER"
               Value="[LocalAppDataFolder]Programs\Shellvis"
               Condition="SCOPE = &quot;user&quot;" Order="2" />

      <Publish Dialog="ScopeDlg" Control="Next" Property="INSTALLFOLDER"
               Value="[ProgramFiles64Folder]Shellvis"
               Condition="SCOPE &lt;&gt; &quot;user&quot;" Order="2" />

      <Publish Dialog="ScopeDlg" Control="Next" Event="SetTargetPath" Value="INSTALLFOLDER"
               Order="3" />

      <Publish Dialog="ScopeDlg" Control="Next" Event="NewDialog" Value="CustomizeDlg"
               Order="4" />

      <Publish Dialog="CustomizeDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="ScopeDlg" Order="10" />

      <Publish Dialog="CustomizeDlg" Control="Next" Event="NewDialog" Value="WhisperDlg" Order="10" />
      <Publish Dialog="WhisperDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="CustomizeDlg" />
      <Publish Dialog="WhisperDlg" Control="Next" Event="NewDialog" Value="VerifyReadyDlg" />
      <Publish Dialog="VerifyReadyDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="WhisperDlg" Order="10" />
    </UI>


    <!--
      One directory id, resolved at RUN time rather than at build time.
      WixUI_Advanced owns INSTALLFOLDER: its install-scope page sets ALLUSERS and then
      points INSTALLFOLDER at WixPerUserFolder or WixPerMachineFolder accordingly. The
      literal parent below is only the default before that page runs; it is not where a
      per-user installation ends up.
    -->
    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramFiles64Folder">
      <Directory Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Name="Shellvis" />
    </StandardDirectory>

    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramMenuFolder">
      <Directory Id="ShortcutFolder" Name="Shellvis" />
    </StandardDirectory>

    <Feature Id="Application"
             Title="Shellvis"
             Description="The command bar, the console and all tools that need no elevation."
             Level="1"
             AllowAbsent="no">

      <!--
        Harvested rather than listed. A component per file for 450 files is a maintenance
        burden with no benefit here: they install and uninstall together, always, and a
        hand-written list is a list that goes stale the first time a dependency changes.
      -->
      <Files Include="$(Stage)\**" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" />

      <ComponentGroupRef Id="ShortcutComponents" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="AutostartComponents" />
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="SetupChoiceComponents" />
    </Feature>

    <Feature Id="BrokerService"
             Title="Privileged broker service"
             Description="Registers the broker as a LocalSystem service, which is what makes elevated operations available. Available only when installing for all users; leave it out and those tools report as unavailable."
             Level="1000">

      <!--
        Absent, not merely unselected, in a per-user installation.
        A service running as LocalSystem is a machine-wide object: it cannot be created
        without elevation and would not belong to the installing user if it could. Offering it
        greyed out would be honest; offering it at all in a context where choosing it must
        fail is not, so at level 0 it disappears from the feature tree entirely.

        MSIINSTALLPERUSER is what the scope page sets, which is why this is a runtime
        condition and not a build-time switch.
      -->
      <Level Value="0" Condition="MSIINSTALLPERUSER" />

      <ComponentGroupRef Id="BrokerComponents" />
    </Feature>

    <ComponentGroup Id="ShortcutComponents" Directory="ShortcutFolder">
      <Component Id="StartMenuShortcut" Guid="*">
        <Shortcut Id="ShellvisShortcut"
                  Name="Shellvis"
                  Description="A native Windows AI agent"
                  Target="[INSTALLFOLDER]Shellvis.Shell.exe"
                  WorkingDirectory="INSTALLFOLDER" />

        <RemoveFolder Id="RemoveShortcutFolder" On="uninstall" />

        <!--
          The key path for a shortcut component has to be a per-user registry value: a
          Start Menu shortcut is not a reliable key path, and HKCU is where a per-user
          package is allowed to write in both scopes.
        -->
        <RegistryValue Root="HKCU" Key="Software\Shellvis" Name="Shortcut" Type="integer" Value="1" KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>

    <ComponentGroup Id="SetupChoiceComponents" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <Component Id="SpeechModelChoice" Guid="*">
        <!--
          The setup answer, recorded where the application looks for it.

          HKCU, so it needs no privilege and is written identically in both installation
          modes. Read by Shellvis as a FALLBACK, not an override: once voice.whisperModel
          appears in config.yaml, that wins; a choice made during setup months ago must
          not quietly overrule the file the user has since edited.
        -->
        <RegistryValue Root="HKCU"
                       Key="Software\Shellvis"
                       Name="WhisperModel"
                       Type="string"
                       Value="[WHISPERMODEL]"
                       KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>

    <ComponentGroup Id="AutostartComponents" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">
      <Component Id="Autostart" Guid="*">
        <!--
          HKCU in BOTH variants, including the machine-wide one. The interactive
          application is per user even when the binaries are shared, and an HKLM Run entry
          would start a copy for every account that logs on to the machine.
        -->
        <RegistryValue Root="HKCU"
                       Key="Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run"
                       Name="Shellvis"
                       Type="string"
                       Value="&quot;[INSTALLFOLDER]Shellvis.Shell.exe&quot;"
                       KeyPath="yes" />
      </Component>
    </ComponentGroup>

      <ComponentGroup Id="BrokerComponents" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER">
        <Component Id="BrokerServiceRegistration" Guid="*">
          <!--
            The broker executable itself comes from the harvest above, so this component
            only registers it, and it needs a key path that is not that file. A registry
            value is the standard answer: a directory as key path is rejected outright by
            the toolset for a component with an auto-generated guid, and duplicating the
            executable into a second component would install it twice.
          -->
          <RegistryValue Root="HKLM"
                         Key="SOFTWARE\Shellvis"
                         Name="BrokerService"
                         Type="integer"
                         Value="1"
                         KeyPath="yes" />

          <ServiceInstall Id="BrokerInstall"
                          Name="ShellvisBroker"
                          DisplayName="Shellvis privileged broker"
                          Description="Carries out the few operations that need administrator rights, on request from the interactive Shellvis session. Refuses everything else."
                          Type="ownProcess"
                          Start="auto"
                          Account="LocalSystem"
                          ErrorControl="normal"
                          Vital="yes"
                          Arguments="--allow-user &quot;[LogonUser]&quot;" />

          <!--
            Stopped and removed on uninstall, and started on install. Without the stop the
            uninstaller cannot delete a running binary and leaves the file behind for a
            reboot.
          -->
          <ServiceControl Id="BrokerControl"
                          Name="ShellvisBroker"
                          Start="install"
                          Stop="both"
                          Remove="uninstall"
                          Wait="yes" />
        </Component>
      </ComponentGroup>

  </Package>
</Wix>
